# Registry tables and bulk actions

Help people find the right records, see what is selected and know how far a bulk change will reach. Entities, devices, areas, labels and categories are related but are not the same thing. Sorting or filtering must not quietly widen the selection.

These examples are local previews with invented records. They do not change a Home Assistant setting. Open an example to see it at full width or read its HTML.

## Scope and native ownership

Home Assistant groups assets with areas, floors, labels, categories and the group integration. A device can have one area. Its entities inherit that area unless you override it, and each entity can have several labels. Labels can also filter tables. A category belongs to one table, so the automations page can have different categories from the scripts page. The native registry screens own selection, sorting and any saved views. The examples are fixed records. They are not a registry or a permission system.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Records loaded | The current filter and sort, and names that mean something | Treat a row for one entity as the whole device |
| Rows selected | The selected count, and whether rows hidden by a filter stay selected | Assume Select all means every record on the server |
| No filter matches | The active criteria and a real way to reset them | Say the registry has no entities |
| Data unavailable or restricted | The actual retrieval or access problem | Describe a failed response as an empty result |
| Partly successful bulk change | The records that succeeded, failed and are not confirmed, listed separately | Write All updated while any result is missing |

## Integration contract

1. Use the stable record IDs and the data the server authorises. Keep the display name apart from the entity ID, which people may need to copy.
2. Before a bulk change, work out the exact set of targets and show the count with a way to review them. Keep the native selection rules through filter, sort and page changes. Describe what Home Assistant does instead of guessing.
3. Offer only the operations every target supports, or say which targets are restricted. Assigning an area, changing a label, disabling and removing have different consequences.
4. A saved filter or view needs the real native storage and scope. A table filtered in the documentation must never claim to have saved an account preference.

## Accessible interaction

- Use real table headers or the native equivalent, show the sort order, and name each row's selection control. The keyboard must not depend on clicking a whole row.
- Announce a changed count of results or selected rows. Do not read out the whole table after each keystroke.
- Keep row actions reachable at narrow widths and with enlarged text. Give people a named way to review the selected rows before anything is deleted or removed.

## Failure and recovery

- If a selected row disappears or a permission changes, check the target set again before running the change, and say what changed.
- After a partial failure, keep the results that succeeded and offer only the retries that are supported. Do not repeat completed work that cannot be undone.
- If the registry cannot load, keep the filter inputs where Home Assistant supports it. Do not show earlier data as if it were a fresh, authorised response.

